      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "The aim of our study is to investigate the bone ongrowth of two different alternative surfaces and the effect of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) on osseointegration."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Hips of 40 New Zealand white rabbits were operated bilaterally. Hydroxyapatite (HA)-coated titanium rods were implanted into the right femur, and grit-blasted titanium rods were implanted into the left femur. They were divided into three groups. At the end of 8 weeks, both femora of the rabbits were removed and investigated biomechanically and histologically."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "HA-coated implants had a significantly better failure load and \"percentage of bone-implant contact\" than grit-blasted implants. There was no significant difference between the medication groups as a result of the biomechanical and histologic investigations."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Our results indicate that NSAIDs did not have any negative effect on the osseointegration. HA-coated implants may provide more tensile strength and greater bone-implant contact rate in comparison with grit-blasted implants."
        }
      ]},
